 \begin{abstract}
-Mesh-to-mesh solution transfer is required whenever an unsteady finite-volume calculation is restarted on a nonmatching mesh, including remeshing, adaptation, and connectivity changes in moving-grid simulations.
-This work compares Taylor-corrected nearest-neighbor interpolation, barycentric interpolation on a simplicial source representation, and Galerkin projection onto a continuous target finite-element space.
-Nearest-neighbor transfer is considered from first to third order, whereas barycentric interpolation and Galerkin projection are considered at second and third order.
-Nearest-neighbor and barycentric transfer are also augmented by a bounded a-posteriori correction of the global lumped mass, momentum, and energy integrals.
-The comparison therefore separates interpolation order, boundedness, consistent Galerkin conservation, solver-lumped conservation, and the effect of enforcing only one domain-wide constraint.
-
-For BDF2 integration, the transfer-induced first-step perturbation is driven by the two-history combination
-\begin{equation*}
-    \eta^n=2\eps^n-\frac{1}{2}\eps^{n-1},
-\end{equation*}
-and its aerodynamic signature is determined by the target-mesh implicit response.
-Static manufactured-field tests recover the nominal orders and distinguish smooth-field accuracy from boundedness and conservation at a discontinuity.
-Repeated transfer in a two-dimensional Euler Riemann problem shows that a global correction may restore the total density integral while leaving cumulative transition displacement and diffusion essentially unchanged.
-In the most aggressive single-realization test, five times more transfers and twice the mesh displacement increase the NN1 and BAR2 transition-position errors to approximately 26 cells, whereas GP2 remains at 0.09 cell.
-
-A steady, shock-free NACA~0012 calculation then validates the restart analysis directly.
-A defect placed only in the older BDF2 history state produces a lift response ten to thirteen times the native target noise floor despite zero error in the latest state, with the sign required by the $-1/2$ coefficient.
-The initial-residual difference agrees with $-M_B\eta^n/\Delta t$ to export precision, and the measured state and lift responses recover the predicted $4/3$ and $-1/3$ history ratios within approximately 1\% in force for all but first-order nearest-neighbor transfer.
-Applying the global correction changes neither the first-step state response nor the lift response in the fourth significant figure.
-A localized one-time transfer additionally produces an acoustic disturbance whose measured pressure-probe speed is $1.18\pm0.08$ chords per time unit, compared with the nondimensional sound speed 1.183.
-Under repeated random remeshing, the numerical drag drift is reproducible across two seeds and is about six times smaller for GP2 than for NN2, although it is not eliminated.
-These results demonstrate that BDF2 restart quality depends on both history states and on the spatial structure of the transfer defect rather than on interpolation order or one global conservation measure alone.
+Remeshing changes both the spatial discretization and the history supplied to a multistep time integrator. These effects complicate the assessment of interpolation accuracy in unsteady flow calculations. This study compares nearest-neighbor Taylor transfer, barycentric interpolation, and continuous Galerkin projection, with nominal smooth-field orders from one to three and optional correction of the solver's global conserved integrals. A target-referenced analysis identifies the BDF2 history forcing as $2\eps^n-\eps^{n-1}/2$ and distinguishes its aerodynamic response from the native discrepancy between meshes. Manufactured fields verify the implemented approximation orders and expose the separate roles of boundedness and integral conservation. Repeated remeshing in a two-dimensional Euler Riemann calculation shows that correcting the total density integral need not reduce transition displacement. In an Euler NACA~0012 calculation, perturbing only the older history produces a nonzero lift response despite an exact latest history. State-response norm ratios agree with the predicted $4/3$ and $1/3$ magnitudes to within 0.3\%, although vector superposition retains a 1.8--3.5\% defect. Across two random-deformation sequences, the numerical-drag trend is approximately 5.8 times smaller with second-order Galerkin projection than with second-order nearest-neighbor transfer. Pressure-probe delays are compatible with acoustic propagation, with threshold-dependent speeds of 1.09--1.28 compared with a freestream sound speed of 1.183. The completed Euler tests isolate mechanisms relevant to remeshed unsteady RANS calculations; they do not yet establish performance for turbulence transport or viscous aerodynamic loads.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
-
-Unsteady finite-volume simulations frequently require the discrete solution to be transferred between nonmatching meshes.
-Examples include anisotropic mesh adaptation, local remeshing, restart on a modified grid, and moving-grid calculations in which the mesh connectivity is regenerated.
-The transfer operation changes the state presented to the target-mesh residual and can therefore introduce a transient response even when the source and target meshes represent the same physical configuration.
-For a multistep time integrator, the issue is compounded because all states in the time-integration history must be transferred consistently.
-
-Solution-transfer procedures are commonly classified by their interpolation order and by whether they preserve the global integrals of the conserved variables.
-Neither property alone determines the quality of a restarted unsteady calculation.
-A high-order pointwise interpolant may introduce a small local error while changing the total mass, momentum, or energy.
-Conversely, imposing a single global conservation constraint removes the integral imbalance but does not necessarily reduce the local residual defect that drives the immediate post-transfer response.
-Galerkin projection preserves the consistent field integral while minimizing the $L_2$ error in the target approximation space, but it requires common-refinement integration and the solution of a target-space mass-matrix system.
-The relevant comparison must therefore distinguish formal accuracy, local approximation, global conservation, boundedness, and computational cost.
-
-The present study considers three transfer families.
-Nearest-neighbor transfer is used in first-, second-, and third-order Taylor forms.
-Barycentric transfer is evaluated in its standard affine-exact second-order form and in a gradient-corrected third-order form.
-The basic forms of both families do not preserve the global finite-volume integrals; each is also tested with an optional bounded redistribution that restores those integrals without changing the underlying local interpolation stencil.
-The third family is an $L_2$ Galerkin projection onto continuous $P^1$ and $P^2$ spaces on the target simplicial mesh, providing second- and third-order variants that conserve the consistent field integral by construction.
-
-The underlying approximation tools are established in the literature.
-The derivative-corrected pointwise methods follow the $k$-exact reconstruction framework developed for unstructured meshes~\cite{BarthFrederickson1990,Barth1993}, while plain barycentric transfer is the standard continuous piecewise-linear interpolation operator on a simplicial mesh~\cite{AlauzetMehrenberger2010,FarrellMaddison2011}.
-Galerkin transfer through source--target intersection integration is treated by Farrell and Maddison~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011}, Farrell~\cite{FarrellThesis2009}, and Jiao and Heath~\cite{JiaoHeath2004}.
-Bounded redistribution of a conservation defect follows the repair and bound-preserving remapping literature~\cite{ShashkovWendroff2004,KucharikShashkovWendroff2003}.
-The contribution of the present work is therefore not the introduction of these base operators in isolation, but their controlled comparison within an unsteady BDF2 restart problem, including the separation of native mesh effects from transfer effects and the distinction between consistent Galerkin conservation and the lumped integral used by a vertex-centered finite-volume solver.
-A specific issue examined for the third-order projection is the effect of retaining only the vertex values of the projected $P^2$ field when the receiving solver has no edge degrees of freedom.
-
-The Galerkin method is introduced directly through its $L_2$ orthogonality condition.
-The source--target common refinement is used only to evaluate the cross-mesh load vector required by that projection.
-The implemented operators are expressed in a common notation, their actual properties are verified numerically, and their errors are related to the restart response of a BDF2 finite-volume calculation.
-Particular attention is paid to the distinction between conservation of the consistent Galerkin integral and conservation of the lumped finite-volume integral used by the downstream solver.
-
-A second distinction is essential when assessing a restart.
-Two simulations advanced from the initial condition on two admissible meshes generally follow different discrete trajectories because their control volumes, residual operators, and force quadratures differ.
-This native mesh-induced discrepancy is present even without interpolation.
-The additional transfer defect is isolated only by comparing the restarted target-mesh trajectory with a trajectory advanced natively on the same target mesh.
-
-The paper addresses four questions:
-\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
-    \item Which polynomial-reproduction, convergence, and conservation properties are achieved by the implemented nearest-neighbor, barycentric, and Galerkin variants on nonmatching unstructured meshes?
-    \item How does the a-posteriori global correction alter the local error, boundedness, and residual compatibility of nearest-neighbor and barycentric transfer?
-    \item How do errors in the two transferred BDF2 history states determine the immediate and delayed response of aerodynamic coefficients?
-    \item Under which conditions do higher order, global conservation correction, or intrinsic Galerkin projection provide a measurable advantage in one-time and repeated transfers?
-\end{enumerate}
-
-The paper first defines the numerical framework and the three transfer families.
-It then derives the BDF2 history-defect response and its projection onto aerodynamic outputs.
-The numerical methodology separates static transfer verification from unsteady flow assessment, after which the results are organized from smooth manufactured fields to subsonic and transonic airfoil applications.
-
-\section{Numerical framework and solution-transfer methods}
+\label{sec:introduction}
+
+Unsteady Reynolds-averaged Navier--Stokes (RANS) calculations use mesh adaptation to follow evolving flow structures without maintaining fine resolution throughout the domain. When adaptation regenerates connectivity, the flow and turbulence histories must be transferred to the new discretization before time integration can continue. The resulting calculation combines spatial approximation, solution transfer, and temporal integration; improving one component does not necessarily reduce their combined error. Time-accurate anisotropic adaptation and space--time adaptation for implicit RANS calculations illustrate this coupling~\cite{AlauzetLoseilleOlivier2018,SauvageAlauzetDervieux2024}.
+
+Two questions arise at each mesh change. Does the transfer represent the source field accurately on the target mesh, and does the transferred state permit a consistent continuation of the target discrete evolution? Polynomial reproduction and integral conservation address the first question only partially. A transfer can preserve a total integral while redistributing the field in a way that changes a residual balance or an aerodynamic load. Conversely, an accurate pointwise interpolant can introduce a small global imbalance. Repeated transfers may accumulate either effect, depending on the error's spatial structure and its interaction with the evolving flow.
+
+These distinctions have precedents beyond compressible aerodynamics. Common-refinement Galerkin projection provides an integral-preserving, best-$L_2$ transfer between finite-element spaces~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011,JiaoHeath2004}. Additional constraints are nevertheless needed to preserve properties such as a discrete norm or weak incompressibility~\cite{MaddisonHiester2017}. Balance-preserving transfer for shallow-water models likewise demonstrates that accurate interpolation and preservation of a steady discrete state are different requirements~\cite{MaddisonCotterFarrell2011,MaddisonCotterFarrell2013}. The present work does not claim that this distinction, Galerkin projection, or polynomial reconstruction is new. It examines their consequences for the two-history restart of a vertex-centered finite-volume BDF2 calculation and for subsequent aerodynamic transients.
+
+The comparison uses three established constructions: nearest-neighbor Taylor transfer, barycentric interpolation on a simplicial representation of the source mesh, and continuous Galerkin projection. Their nominal orders range from one to three. A bounded global correction is also applied to the pointwise methods, allowing the effect of enforcing a total integral to be assessed without replacing the underlying interpolation rule. The analysis distinguishes the consistent finite-element integral from the nodal integral actually used by the receiving solver, particularly when a quadratic projected field is reduced to vertex-only output.
+
+The numerical argument proceeds from static transfer verification to accumulated flow error and then to a controlled BDF2 restart. Manufactured fields test accuracy and boundedness independently of a flow solver. A two-dimensional Euler Riemann calculation tests repeated transfer under changes in frequency and deformation amplitude. A steady Euler NACA~0012 case uses independently converged source and target solutions to test the contribution of each BDF2 history; separate random-deformation and localized-transfer experiments assess load drift and delayed response. Euler flow is used to isolate these mechanisms from turbulence closure. The intended application is remeshed unsteady RANS, but the completed evidence supports conclusions about the common transfer and time-integration mechanisms, not yet about viscous stresses, turbulence variables, or separated RANS dynamics.
+
+\section{Solution-transfer operators}
 \label{sec:numerical-framework}
 
-\subsection{Unsteady finite-volume discretization}
+\subsection{Representation and reconstructed derivatives}
+\label{sec:derivative-reconstruction}
+
+The source and target meshes, $A$ and $B$, cover the same discretized domain $\Omega$. Their nodal values are $U_A$ and $U_B$, and $M_M=\operatorname{diag}(w_i^M)\otimes I$ contains the positive control-volume weights of the vertex-centered flow solver on mesh $M$. For one component $\phi$, the solver's integral is
+\begin{equation}
+ Q_M(\phi_M)=\sum_i w_i^M\phi_{M,i}.
+ \label{eq:fv-discrete-integral}
+\end{equation}
+All compared transfers return values at the solver vertices. Density, momentum, and total energy are transferred in conserved-variable form; nonlinear products are not reconstructed from separately interpolated primitive variables. Nodal sampling and control-volume averaging are different representation operators, even when the same weights are used to monitor an integral.
+
+Nearest-neighbor corrections and the quadratic source representation require derivatives recovered from surrounding nodal data. A weighted least-squares fit determines the coefficients in
+\begin{equation}
+ \phi_j-\phi_i\simeq g_i\cdot r_{ij}
+       +\tfrac12r_{ij}^{T}H_i r_{ij},\qquad r_{ij}=x_j-x_i.
+ \label{eq:wls-polynomial}
+\end{equation}
+The degree-one fit omits the Hessian; the degree-two fit retains it. With a full-rank, suitably scaled stencil and smooth data, polynomial reproduction gives an $O(h)$ gradient error for the linear fit and $O(h^2)$ gradient and $O(h)$ Hessian errors for the quadratic fit. These are conditional estimates: irregularity, anisotropy, rank deficiency, and weighting can degrade derivative recovery~\cite{BarthFrederickson1990,Barth1993,Mavriplis2003,DiskinThomas2008}. The third-order variants therefore use degree-two reconstruction. Their accuracy is checked numerically rather than inferred from the requested polynomial degree alone.
+
+\subsection{Nearest-neighbor and barycentric interpolation}
+\label{sec:point-transfer}
+
+Nearest-neighbor transfer selects the closest source vertex $n(j)$ to each target vertex and evaluates a Taylor expansion there. With $r_j=x_j^B-x_{n(j)}^A$, the three variants are
+\begin{align}
+ (\calI^{\mathrm{NN1}}_{AB}\phi_A)_j&=\phi_{A,n(j)},\label{eq:nn1}\\
+ (\calI^{\mathrm{NN2}}_{AB}\phi_A)_j&=\phi_{A,n(j)}+g_{n(j)}\cdot r_j,\label{eq:nn2}\\
+ (\calI^{\mathrm{NN3}}_{AB}\phi_A)_j&=\phi_{A,n(j)}+g_{n(j)}\cdot r_j
+                 +\tfrac12r_j^TH_{n(j)}r_j.\label{eq:nn3}
+\end{align}
+Thus NN1 copies a value, NN2 accounts for local slope, and NN3 also accounts for curvature. No containing-element search is required. For $|r_j|=O(h)$ and accurate derivatives, their smooth-field errors are respectively $O(h)$, $O(h^2)$, and $O(h^3)$. The selected donor changes discontinuously across source-node Voronoi boundaries, so a smooth displacement need not produce a smooth change in the interpolation error.
+
+Barycentric interpolation instead locates the source simplex containing the target point. A conforming triangulated or tetrahedralized view of the original mesh supplies this search geometry, using original vertices rather than an augmented control-volume point set. If the containing simplex has vertices $x_{i_r}^A$, its barycentric coordinates satisfy
+\begin{equation}
+ x_j^B=\sum_{r=1}^{d+1}\lambda_r x_{i_r}^A,
+ \qquad \sum_r\lambda_r=1,\qquad \lambda_r\geq0.
+ \label{eq:barycentric-coordinates}
+\end{equation}
+They express the target point as a geometric weighted average of the simplex vertices. Applying the same weights to the field gives
+\begin{align}
+ (\calI^{\mathrm{BAR2}}_{AB}\phi_A)_j
+ &=\sum_r\lambda_r\phi_{A,i_r},\label{eq:bar2}\\
+ (\calI^{\mathrm{BAR3}}_{AB}\phi_A)_j
+ &=\sum_r\lambda_r\left[\phi_{A,i_r}
+       +\tfrac12 g_{i_r}\cdot(x_j^B-x_{i_r}^A)\right].\label{eq:bar3}
+\end{align}
+\label{sec:barycentric-transfer}
+BAR2 is affine-exact and already second order. For a quadratic field with exact vertex gradients, the half-gradient term in BAR3 cancels the quadratic interpolation remainder, yielding third-order consistency. The factor $1/2$ follows from this cancellation rather than from calibration. Both expressions are continuous across a conforming simplex interface when shared vertices have unique values and gradients. Neither enforces Eq.~\eqref{eq:fv-discrete-integral} between meshes.
+
+Limiting is separate from nominal order. A factor $0\leq\Phi_i\leq1$ may scale both derivative terms in NN3, so $\Phi_i=0$ recovers NN1; in BAR3 it scales the gradient correction and recovers BAR2, not a piecewise-constant interpolant. The smooth verification uses unlimited reconstruction. The discontinuous tests compare unlimited and Barth--Jespersen-limited transfer~\cite{BarthJespersen1989}; differentiable and higher-order limiter constructions provide related alternatives~\cite{Venkatakrishnan1995,MichalakOllivierGooch2009,Nishikawa2022}. Damping a quadratic with a factor chosen from linear increments alone does not establish boundedness at every evaluation point. The measured extrema and the final thermodynamic admissibility of the state remain necessary diagnostics.
+
+\subsection{Galerkin projection and common-refinement integration}
+\label{sec:galerkin-projection}
+
+Galerkin projection determines a coupled target field rather than evaluating each target point independently. Let $V_B^p$ be the continuous degree-$p$ Lagrange space on the target simplicial mesh. The projection of a source representation $\phi_A^h$ satisfies
+\begin{equation}
+ (\phi_B^h-\phi_A^h,v_B)_{L_2(\Omega)}=0
+ \quad\forall v_B\in V_B^p,
+ \label{eq:galerkin-orthogonality}
+\end{equation}
+and minimizes the $L_2$ distance to that source representation~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011,JiaoHeath2004}. For $\phi_B^h=\sum_jv_jN_j^B$, this gives
+\begin{equation}
+ H_Bv=b,\qquad
+ (H_B)_{ij}=\int_\Omega N_i^BN_j^B\dd x,\qquad
+ b_i=\int_\Omega N_i^B\phi_A^h\dd x.
+ \label{eq:galerkin-system}
+\end{equation}
+The consistent mass matrix $H_B$ is sparse and positive definite. Its off-diagonal entries couple basis functions sharing target elements; solving the system determines all coefficients simultaneously. It is distinct from the diagonal finite-volume matrix $M_B$ used in time integration.
+
+The load vector is the cross-mesh operation. On one target element, $N_i^B$ has a fixed polynomial expression, but the source field may cross several source-element boundaries and change polynomial expression within that element. Consequently,
+\begin{equation}
+ b_i=\sum_{K_B}\sum_{K_A:\,|K_A\cap K_B|>0}
+       \int_{K_A\cap K_B}N_i^B\phi_A^h\dd x.
+ \label{eq:galerkin-load-intersections}
+\end{equation}
+Each intersection fixes both polynomial expressions and permits exact polynomial quadrature. These intersections form the source--target common refinement; they may be integrated locally without storing a complete additional mesh. Coverage must be complete and nonoverlapping. Products require degree-two quadrature for $P^1$/$P^1$ transfer and degree-four quadrature for $P^2$/$P^2$ transfer on affine simplices~\cite{Dunavant1985,Keast1986}. This geometric integration and the mass-matrix solve distinguish projection from the point searches in NN and BAR.
+
+GP2 uses $P^1$ source and target spaces. GP3 uses $P^2$ spaces, introducing edge coefficients internally. With $d_{ij}=x_i-x_j$ and $\overline H_{ij}=(H_i+H_j)/2$, the source midpoint value is reconstructed as
+\begin{equation}
+ \phi_{ij}=\tfrac12(\phi_i+\phi_j)
+                 -\tfrac18d_{ij}^T\overline H_{ij}d_{ij}.
+ \label{eq:gp3-edge-value}
+\end{equation}
+For a quadratic, the subtracted curvature term is exactly the difference between the endpoint average and the midpoint value. GP3 then solves for the complete quadratic target field, but only its original-vertex values are supplied to the flow solver. Nominal $L_2$ approximation orders are two and three under the usual smoothness and mesh-stability assumptions; nodal accuracy after this complete pipeline is assessed separately.
+
+\label{sec:simplicial-representation}
+On mixed-element grids, BAR and GP use conforming subdivisions based on a deterministic shared-face diagonal rule. The approximation is $P^p$ on the simplices, not $Q^p$ on the native quadrilaterals or hexahedra. A valid conforming split and the chosen control-volume weights must therefore be retained consistently in geometric and integral checks.
+
+\subsection{Which integral is conserved?}
+\label{sec:global-correction}
+\label{sec:galerkin-vertex-output}
+
+For an unconstrained target space containing constants, Eq.~\eqref{eq:galerkin-orthogonality} preserves the integral of the complete finite-element field. Coordinate functions also belong to continuous $P^1$, and quadratic coordinates belong to $P^2$. Exact projection therefore preserves the corresponding consistent moments before constraints, sampling, or limiting. This is not finite-volume conservation over arbitrary target cells: their indicator functions generally do not belong to the continuous test space. Nor is the inverse mass matrix a local transfer operator. Equality of source and target geometry on one subregion does not by itself make the projection the identity there.
+
+For GP2, the consistent and solver-lumped integrals coincide when $w_i^M=\int N_i^M\dd x$ on both meshes. For GP3, source lifting and target vertex restriction each introduce a representation-dependent difference. In particular, a third-order projected field does not imply an $O(h^3)$ lumped-integral defect. Appendix~\ref{app:quadrature-mismatch} gives a quadratic counterexample with an $O(h^2)$ discrepancy. The consistent projection, the stored restart field, and any repaired field are consequently distinguished throughout the comparison.
+
+A global correction can enforce a chosen solver integral independently of the transfer construction. For a raw nodal field $\phi_B^r$, it seeks a correction $c$ such that
+\begin{equation}
+ \sum_j w_j^B c_j=Q_A(\phi_A)-Q_B(\phi_B^r),
+ \qquad \ell_j\leq\phi_{B,j}^r+c_j\leq u_j.
+ \label{eq:global-correction-constraint}
+\end{equation}
+The implemented repair redistributes the defect over nodes with available capacity, freezing saturated nodes~\cite{ShashkovWendroff2004,KucharikShashkovWendroff2003}. Feasibility requires the requested integral to lie between the weighted lower and upper bounds, with fixed boundary values included. Source-range bounds on individual conserved components do not alone guarantee positive pressure. Accuracy is retained only if the correction is sufficiently small in the accuracy norm; a small total defect does not itself bound every nodal increment.
+
+\label{sec:galerkin-limiter}
+The optional NN/BAR correction is denoted by the suffix GC. GP additionally uses a projection-level limiter: source-vertex values in overlapping elements define $[\ell_j,u_j]$, widened by $\tau$ times the global source range. Clipping is followed by redistribution preserving a selected lumped total. Hard limiting sets $\tau=0$; soft limiting allows a controlled excursion. Once active, this nonlinear post-processing need not preserve Galerkin orthogonality, first moments, or the best-approximation property. For GP2, preserving its lumped total preserves its consistent mass only under the weight compatibility above. For vertex-only GP3, no such implication holds automatically. These qualifications matter when interpreting the method labels in the numerical results.
+
+\section{BDF2 response to a mesh change}
+\label{sec:restart-consistency}
+
+\subsection{Reference trajectories and history mismatch}
 \label{sec:unsteady-discretization}
-
-Let $U_M(t)$ denote the discrete state vector on mesh $M$, with the diagonal matrix $M_M$ containing the control-volume weights used by the finite-volume solver.
-The semi-discrete governing equations are written as
-\begin{equation}
-    M_M\frac{\dd U_M}{\dd t}+F_M(U_M,t)=0,
-    \label{eq:semidiscrete}
-\end{equation}
-where $M_M$ is the diagonal control-volume matrix and $F_M$ denotes the spatial residual associated with the selected governing equations. It contains the inviscid and boundary contributions and, where applicable, viscous, turbulence-model, transition-model, source, and moving-grid terms.
-For moving-grid calculations, the arbitrary-Lagrangian--Eulerian and metric terms are included in $F_M$ and are assumed to satisfy the geometric-conservation requirements of the underlying solver.
-The transfer considered below occurs between two meshes representing the same physical configuration at the transfer time.
-
-With a constant physical time step $\Delta t$, the target-mesh BDF2 update is
-\begin{equation}
-    \frac{1}{\Delta t}M_B
-    \left(
-        \frac{3}{2}U_B^{n+1}-2U_B^n+\frac{1}{2}U_B^{n-1}
-    \right)
-    +F_B(U_B^{n+1},t^{n+1})=0.
-    \label{eq:bdf2}
-\end{equation}
-The nonlinear system is assumed to be converged sufficiently at each physical time step so that inner-iteration errors remain smaller than the transfer effects under investigation.
-
-\subsection{Discrete data and transfer requirements}
-
-Let $\mathcal{M}_A$ and $\mathcal{M}_B$ denote the source and target meshes, with vertex locations $x_i^A$ and $x_j^B$.
-The transfer operators considered in this work return nodal values on the target mesh.
-The downstream node-centered finite-volume solver associates each nodal state with a control-volume weight $|C_i^M|$ and evaluates the lumped discrete integral
-\begin{equation}
-    I_M^{\mathrm{FV}}(\phi_M)
-    =
-    \bm{1}^TM_M\phi_M
-    =
-    \sum_i \abs{C_i^M}\phi_{M,i}.
-    \label{eq:fv-discrete-integral}
-\end{equation}
-For the conserved flow state, density, momentum, and total energy are transferred componentwise in their conserved-variable form.
-Products such as momentum are formed before transfer rather than reconstructed from independently transferred primitive variables.
-
-For nearest-neighbor and barycentric interpolation, global conservation is assessed directly using the source and target finite-volume integrals,
-\begin{equation}
-    E_{\mathrm{cons}}^{\mathrm{FV}}(U_A)
-    =
-    \bm{1}^TM_B\calI_{AB}U_A-\bm{1}^TM_AU_A.
-    \label{eq:conservation-error}
-\end{equation}
-The optional correction introduced below enforces this lumped finite-volume constraint.
-
-Galerkin projection conserves a related but not always identical quantity.
-If $\phi_B^h$ is the projected finite-element field, Galerkin orthogonality preserves the consistent integral
-\begin{equation}
-    I_B^{\mathrm{G}}(\phi_B^h)
-    =
-    \int_{\Omega}\phi_B^h\dd x.
-    \label{eq:galerkin-integral}
-\end{equation}
-For a $P^1$ target space, the consistent integral coincides with the lumped nodal integral when the finite-volume weights are generated from the same simplicial partition.
-For a vertex-only restart obtained from a $P^2$ projection, the discarded edge degrees of freedom generally make the two integrals differ by the order of the projection error.
-Both measures are therefore reported for the Galerkin variants rather than using a single conservation metric for two different discrete integrals.
-
-Additional transported variables may not possess a global invariant, but their interpolation, limiting, clipping, and positivity treatment must still be reported because they contribute to the BDF2 restart perturbation.
-
-\subsection{Derivative reconstruction and nonlinear stabilization}
-\label{sec:derivative-reconstruction}
-
-The first-order nearest-neighbor method and the standard barycentric method use only the source nodal values.
-The higher-order variants additionally require information on how the solution varies around each source node.
-This information is not directly stored by the node-centered flow solver and must therefore be reconstructed from neighboring values.
-
-The reconstruction is obtained through a weighted least-squares fit.
-Conceptually, a local polynomial is chosen so that its values at the neighboring nodes approximate the available discrete data as closely as possible.
-At a source point $x_i$, the fitted polynomial is written as
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi(x_j)-\phi_i
-    \simeq
-    g_i\cdot(x_j-x_i)
-    +\frac{1}{2}(x_j-x_i)^TH_i(x_j-x_i),
-    \label{eq:wls-polynomial}
-\end{equation}
-where $g_i$ and $H_i$ are the reconstructed gradient and Hessian.
-Neighboring equations are weighted according to distance so that nearby data have a greater influence on the fit.
-The reconstruction does not transfer the solution by itself; it supplies the derivatives used by NN2, NN3, BAR3, and GP3.
-
-A degree-one fit retains only the linear term and provides the gradients used by the nominal second-order methods.
-A degree-two fit retains both terms and is used for every third-order variant.
-The latter choice is necessary because a third-order value reconstruction requires both a Hessian and a gradient whose own error is sufficiently small.
-This is the usual $k$-exact reconstruction logic: a degree-$k$ polynomial fit reproduces polynomials through degree $k$, while the accuracy of the recovered derivatives depends on stencil regularity, size, and weighting~\cite{BarthFrederickson1990,Barth1993,Mavriplis2003,DiskinThomas2008}.
-Stencil construction, distance weighting, rank checks, and any fallback to a lower-degree fit are part of the numerical method and are kept identical across transfer families wherever possible.
-This common reconstruction prevents differences between schemes from being confused with differences in derivative recovery.
-
-Unlimited derivatives are used in the smooth manufactured-field tests so that the nominal interpolation order can be measured directly.
-For transonic or otherwise under-resolved solutions, however, extrapolating a polynomial from neighboring values may create new extrema, negative density, or negative pressure.
-A node-based limiter may then scale the reconstructed Taylor increment before it is used by NN2, NN3, or BAR3.
-For a limiter factor $\Phi_i\in[0,1]$, the second-order reconstruction becomes
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi_i+\Phi_i g_i\cdot(x-x_i),
-\end{equation}
-and the third-order reconstruction uses the same factor for both derivative terms,
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi_i
-    +\Phi_i g_i\cdot(x-x_i)
-    +\frac{1}{2}\Phi_i(x-x_i)^TH_i(x-x_i).
-    \label{eq:limited-quadratic-reconstruction}
-\end{equation}
-Thus $\Phi_i\rightarrow0$ collapses the entire reconstruction to the bounded nodal value rather than leaving an unrestricted Hessian term active near a discontinuity.
-Classical choices include the Barth--Jespersen limiter~\cite{BarthJespersen1989}, the differentiable Venkatakrishnan limiter~\cite{Venkatakrishnan1995}, and higher-order accuracy-preserving limiter functions~\cite{MichalakOllivierGooch2009,Nishikawa2022}.
-The limiter is treated as a separate stabilization choice rather than as part of the formal order definition: in smooth regions it should remain inactive, whereas close to a shock it may deliberately reduce a nominally high-order reconstruction toward a lower-order bounded one.
-Its type, parameters, activation frequency, and any additional positivity correction are therefore reported separately.
-Galerkin projection does not possess a per-node Taylor increment and is bounded by the projection-level treatment introduced below.
-
-\subsection{Point-value transfer methods}
-\label{sec:point-transfer}
-
-Nearest-neighbor and barycentric interpolation are \emph{direct point-evaluation methods}.
-For every target node they identify a small amount of source data and evaluate a local approximation there.
-Consequently, the target nodes are processed independently: no equation couples the value assigned to one target node with the values assigned to the others.
-This locality makes both families comparatively simple and inexpensive, but it also means that neither family preserves a global integral unless the separate correction of Section~\ref{sec:global-correction} is applied.
-
-\subsubsection{Nearest-neighbor Taylor transfer}
-
-The nearest-neighbor family starts from the simplest possible correspondence between two nonmatching meshes.
-For each target node, the geometrically closest source node is selected as the donor.
-The first-order method copies the donor value directly.
-The higher-order variants interpret the donor value as the center of a local Taylor expansion and use reconstructed derivatives to estimate how the field changes over the displacement from the donor node to the target node.
-Thus, the three variants use the same nearest-node search and differ only in how much local variation is reconstructed after the donor has been selected.
-
-For a target point $x_j^B$, define
-\begin{equation}
-    n(j)=\arg\min_i\abs{x_j^B-x_i^A},
-    \qquad
-    \delta_j=x_j^B-x_{n(j)}^A.
-\end{equation}
-The three nearest-neighbor variants are
+\label{sec:native-transfer-decomposition}
+
+Between remeshing events, the mesh is fixed and the semi-discrete equations are
+\begin{equation}
+ M_M\dot U_M+F_M(U_M,t)=0.
+ \label{eq:semidiscrete}
+\end{equation}
+The residual can contain Euler, viscous, or RANS terms; the state must include every transported variable required by the time integrator. The constant-step BDF2 equation on the target mesh is
+\begin{equation}
+ \Phi_B(Y;V,W)=\frac{M_B}{\Delta t}
+       \left(\tfrac32Y-2V+\tfrac12W\right)+F_B(Y,t^{n+1})=0.
+ \label{eq:bdf2}
+\end{equation}
+This form is not a general moving-volume ALE formula. Continuously moving meshes require consistent geometric history terms and a discrete geometric conservation law~\cite{KoobusFarhat1999,GeuzaineGrandmontFarhat2003}; those are outside the fixed-mesh-between-transfers derivation.
+
+Let $U_A^k$ and $U_B^k$ be native trajectories for the same physical problem, computed on the two meshes. The transferred histories and their target-referenced discrepancies are
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat U_B^k=\calI_{AB}(U_A^k),\qquad
+ \eps^k=\widehat U_B^k-U_B^k,\qquad k=n,n-1.
+ \label{eq:history-defects}
+\end{equation}
+Here $\calI_{AB}$ includes all limiting, correction, and restart-field processing. Let $\Pi_Mu^k$ represent a common exact field in the solver's chosen data representation, and $e_M^k=U_M^k-\Pi_Mu^k$. An exact decomposition, valid also for nonlinear transfer, is
 \begin{align}
-    (\calI_{AB}^{\mathrm{NN1}}\phi_A)_j
-    &=\phi_{A,n(j)},
-    \label{eq:nn1}\\
-    (\calI_{AB}^{\mathrm{NN2}}\phi_A)_j
-    &=\phi_{A,n(j)}+g_{n(j)}\cdot\delta_j,
-    \label{eq:nn2}\\
-    (\calI_{AB}^{\mathrm{NN3}}\phi_A)_j
-    &=\phi_{A,n(j)}+g_{n(j)}\cdot\delta_j
-      +\frac{1}{2}\delta_j^TH_{n(j)}\delta_j.
-    \label{eq:nn3}
+ \eps^k={}&\underbrace{\calI_{AB}(\Pi_Au^k)-\Pi_Bu^k}_{\text{transfer consistency}}\notag\\
+ &+\underbrace{\calI_{AB}(U_A^k)-\calI_{AB}(\Pi_Au^k)-e_B^k}_{\text{propagated source error and target error}}.
+ \label{eq:defect-decomposition}
 \end{align}
-NN1 therefore assumes that the solution is locally constant around the donor node.
-NN2 adds the linear change predicted by the donor gradient, and NN3 also accounts for local curvature through the Hessian.
-For smooth pointwise data and locally comparable meshes, the corresponding nominal errors are $O(h)$, $O(h^2)$, and $O(h^3)$, provided the reconstructed derivatives have the required accuracy.
-
-The method does not require identification of a source element containing the target point.
-This makes it robust when source elements are highly irregular or when point location is expensive.
-Its main geometric weakness is that the selected donor changes abruptly when a target point crosses a boundary between two source-node Voronoi regions.
-Even if the underlying solution and mesh motion are smooth, the interpolation stencil can therefore change discontinuously.
-This property is particularly relevant to repeated transfers and to continuously changing meshes, where it may introduce small temporal irregularities.
-The basic NN operators do not enforce Eq.~\eqref{eq:conservation-error}.
-The construction can be viewed as a $k$-exact Taylor reconstruction evaluated at the target point, with NN1 corresponding to the zeroth-order nearest-neighbor limit~\cite{BarthFrederickson1990,Barth1993,Shepard1968}.
-
-\subsubsection{Barycentric transfer}
-\label{sec:barycentric-transfer}
-
-Barycentric interpolation uses more geometric information than nearest-neighbor transfer.
-Instead of selecting one donor node, it first locates the source triangle in two dimensions, or source tetrahedron in three dimensions, that contains the target point.
-The target point is then represented as a weighted combination of the simplex vertices.
-The same weights are applied to the field values at those vertices.
-In this sense, the method reconstructs a polynomial over the containing simplex and evaluates that polynomial at the target point.
-
-Let $\mathcal{S}_A$ be a conforming triangulation of the original source mesh in two dimensions or a conforming tetrahedralization in three dimensions.
-This simplicial view is used only for point location and interpolation; no finite-volume control-volume construction enters the method.
-For a containing simplex $S$ with vertices $x_{i_r}^A$, $r=1,\ldots,d+1$, the barycentric coordinates are defined by
-\begin{equation}
-    x_j^B=\sum_{r=1}^{d+1}\lambda_r x_{i_r}^A,
-    \qquad
-    \sum_{r=1}^{d+1}\lambda_r=1.
-    \label{eq:barycentric-coordinates}
-\end{equation}
-For a point inside a nondegenerate simplex, the coordinates satisfy $0\leq\lambda_r\leq1$.
-They can therefore be interpreted as geometric weights: a target point close to one vertex receives a larger contribution from that vertex, while a point on a face receives contributions only from the vertices of that face.
-
-The standard barycentric interpolant is
-\begin{equation}
-    (\calI_{AB}^{\mathrm{BAR2}}\phi_A)_j
-    =
-    \sum_{r=1}^{d+1}\lambda_r\phi_{A,i_r}.
-    \label{eq:bar2}
-\end{equation}
-Because the barycentric coordinates reproduce both constants and coordinates, this expression reproduces every affine field exactly.
-It is therefore nominally second order for smooth pointwise data on a shape-regular simplicial mesh.
-There is no separate first-order barycentric member: the basic construction is already a linear interpolant.
-
-BAR3 augments the value supplied by each simplex vertex with a derivative-based estimate of the variation between that vertex and the target point,
-\begin{equation}
-    (\calI_{AB}^{\mathrm{BAR3}}\phi_A)_j
-    =
-    \sum_{r=1}^{d+1}\lambda_r
-    \left[
-        \phi_{A,i_r}
-        +\frac{1}{2}g_{i_r}\cdot(x_j^B-x_{i_r}^A)
-    \right].
-    \label{eq:bar3}
-\end{equation}
-The factor $1/2$ is not an empirical relaxation parameter.
-With exact vertex gradients, it cancels the leading quadratic error of the standard barycentric interpolant, yielding a quadratic-exact and nominally third-order construction.
-BAR3 consequently uses the degree-two least-squares gradients of Section~\ref{sec:derivative-reconstruction}.
-
-Unlike NN, barycentric interpolation changes continuously while the target point remains inside the same source simplex.
-Its stencil changes when the point crosses a source-element boundary, but BAR2 remains continuous across a conforming simplicial mesh because adjacent elements share the same nodal values on their common face.
-BAR3 is also continuous when a unique reconstructed gradient is associated with each shared vertex: on the common face, the barycentric coordinate of every vertex outside that face is zero, and both adjacent simplices reduce to the same weighted expression over the shared vertices.
-The derivative of the reconstructed field may nevertheless be discontinuous across element boundaries, as expected for a continuous piecewise-polynomial representation.
-Neither barycentric variant preserves the lumped global integral before correction.
-For target points outside the source simplicial coverage, or for ambiguous and degenerate simplices, the fallback rule must be stated and included in the accuracy and conservation statistics.
-Plain barycentric transfer is the standard consistent $P^1$ interpolation operator on a simplicial mesh; its lack of global conservation and its difference from an $L_2$ projection are discussed in the solution-transfer literature~\cite{AlauzetMehrenberger2010,FarrellMaddison2011}.
-
-\subsection{A-posteriori global conservation correction}
-\label{sec:global-correction}
-
-The optional global correction is applied \emph{after} NN or barycentric interpolation has produced a complete target field.
-It does not change which source node or simplex was used at any target point.
-Instead, it measures the single scalar imbalance between the source and target discrete integrals and redistributes that imbalance over the target nodes.
-The purpose is to distinguish two questions that would otherwise be mixed together: how accurately the local interpolant reconstructs the field, and whether the final target field preserves the total amount of a conserved variable.
-
-Let $\phi_B^{\mathrm{raw}}=\calI_{AB}\phi_A$ be the uncorrected target field and define
-\begin{equation}
-    \mathcal{D}
-    =
-    I_A^{\mathrm{FV}}(\phi_A)-I_B^{\mathrm{FV}}(\phi_B^{\mathrm{raw}}).
-    \label{eq:global-defect}
-\end{equation}
-If $\mathcal{D}>0$, the raw target field contains too little of the considered quantity; if $\mathcal{D}<0$, it contains too much.
-The corrected field is written as
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi_B^{\mathrm{GC}}
-    =
-    \calK_B(\phi_B^{\mathrm{raw}})
-    =
-    \phi_B^{\mathrm{raw}}+\delta,
-    \label{eq:global-corrected-field}
-\end{equation}
-where the correction vector satisfies the single global constraint
-\begin{equation}
-    \bm{1}^TM_B\delta=\mathcal{D}.
-    \label{eq:global-correction-constraint}
-\end{equation}
-Many different vectors satisfy this equation.
-The redistribution algorithm selects one that changes the raw field only as much as needed while respecting admissible bounds,
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi_{\min}^{A}
-    \le
-    \phi_{B,j}^{\mathrm{raw}}+\delta_j
-    \le
-    \phi_{\max}^{A}.
-    \label{eq:global-correction-bounds}
-\end{equation}
-Here $\phi_{\min}^{A}$ and $\phi_{\max}^{A}$ denote the selected lower and upper source bounds.
-Nodes with greater available distance to the active bound are allowed to absorb a larger share of the defect.
-When a node reaches a bound, it is removed from the active set and the remaining defect is redistributed over the unsaturated nodes.
-The process continues until Eq.~\eqref{eq:global-correction-constraint} is satisfied to the prescribed tolerance or no admissible capacity remains.
-
-This operation restores only the \emph{total} integral.
-For example, it may compensate a local excess near the airfoil by a small correction distributed over a much larger part of the domain.
-It therefore does not create local conservation, does not guarantee a smaller target residual, and does not necessarily reduce the BDF2 history defect relevant to a force response.
-These limitations are central to the paper: the corrected and uncorrected variants have the same underlying interpolation stencil, but differ in one global constraint.
-
-Density, momentum components, and total energy are corrected in their conserved-variable form.
-The components may be corrected independently, but the final state must also satisfy positivity and any additional thermodynamic admissibility conditions.
-If these conditions require a coupled correction, that procedure is documented explicitly.
-Under a stable redistribution whose magnitude is of the same order as the raw conservation defect, the nominal convergence order of the base interpolant should be retained.
-This is verified numerically, especially in cases where the bounds become active.
-The corrected variants are denoted NN1-GC, NN2-GC, NN3-GC, BAR2-GC, and BAR3-GC.
-The active-set redistribution follows the general repair paradigm for enforcing conservation without violating admissible bounds~\cite{ShashkovWendroff2004}; related linearity- and bound-preserving redistribution strategies are described by Kucharik et al.~\cite{KucharikShashkovWendroff2003} and originate from flux-corrected transport ideas~\cite{Zalesak1979}.
-
-\subsection{Galerkin projection}
-\label{sec:galerkin-projection}
-
-Galerkin projection approaches the transfer problem differently from NN and barycentric interpolation.
-The pointwise methods determine each target value from a local source stencil.
-Galerkin projection instead seeks one target finite-element field whose global $L_2$ distance from the source field is as small as possible.
-Common-refinement Galerkin transfer between nonmatching meshes is established in both solution-transfer and multiphysics-coupling settings~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011,FarrellThesis2009,JiaoHeath2004}.
-The unknowns are still nodal coefficients, but they are determined simultaneously because changing one coefficient modifies the approximation over every target element that contains its basis function.
-
-Both source and target meshes are represented by conforming simplicial refinements constructed from the existing mesh vertices.
-Let $V_B^p$ be the continuous Lagrange finite-element space of degree $p$ on the target simplicial mesh, with basis functions $\{N_i\}$.
-The projected field $\phi_B^h\in V_B^p$ is characterized by
-\begin{equation}
-    \int_{\Omega}(\phi_B^h-\phi_A^h)v_B\dd x=0
-    \qquad
-    \forall v_B\in V_B^p,
-    \label{eq:galerkin-orthogonality}
-\end{equation}
-where $\phi_A^h$ is the selected source representation.
-This condition states that the remaining projection error has zero weighted average against every target basis function.
-Equivalently, no admissible perturbation within the target space can reduce the squared $L_2$ error further; the result is the best approximation of the source field available in that space.
-
-Writing $\phi_B^h=\sum_jv_jN_j$ and choosing each basis function in turn as the test function gives
-\begin{equation}
-    M^{\mathrm{G}}v=b,
-    \qquad
-    M_{ij}^{\mathrm{G}}=\int_{\Omega}N_iN_j\dd x,
-    \qquad
-    b_i=\int_{\Omega}N_i\phi_A^h\dd x.
-    \label{eq:galerkin-system}
-\end{equation}
-The matrix $M^{\mathrm{G}}$ is the consistent mass matrix of the target finite-element space.
-It is sparse because two basis functions interact only when their supports overlap, and it is symmetric positive definite for the spaces considered here.
-Unlike a pointwise interpolation formula, Eq.~\eqref{eq:galerkin-system} couples neighboring target coefficients; the complete target field is obtained by solving this sparse system.
-
-The right-hand side $b$ is the only part that combines the two meshes.
-To understand its assembly, consider one target element $K_B$ and one overlapping source element $K_A$.
-Inside $K_B$, the target basis function $N_i$ has one fixed polynomial expression.
-Inside $K_A$, the source field $\phi_A^h$ has one fixed polynomial expression.
-Because source and target element boundaries do not generally coincide, a single target element may intersect several source elements, and $\phi_A^h$ may therefore change polynomial expression within that target element.
-The integral is consequently split over the intersection regions
-\begin{equation}
-    P_{AB}=K_A\cap K_B,
-\end{equation}
-so that
-\begin{equation}
-    b_i
-    =
-    \sum_{K_B}
-    \sum_{K_A:\,K_A\cap K_B\neq\emptyset}
-    \int_{K_A\cap K_B}N_i\phi_A^h\dd x.
-    \label{eq:galerkin-load-intersections}
-\end{equation}
-On each intersection region, both factors are represented by known polynomials and can be integrated with the quadrature order required by their product.
-The collection of these intersections is referred to as the source--target common refinement.
-A complete global refinement mesh need not be stored: the overlapping element pairs and their intersection geometry may be generated, integrated, and discarded locally during assembly.
-The essential requirement is that the overlap decomposition be geometrically complete and nonoverlapping, so that the cross-mesh integral is evaluated without gaps or double counting.
-
-The Galerkin algorithm can therefore be summarized as follows.
-First, construct the source and target finite-element representations.
-Second, assemble the target mass matrix, which depends only on the target mesh.
-Third, find each overlapping source--target element pair and integrate its contribution to the load vector.
-Finally, solve Eq.~\eqref{eq:galerkin-system} for all target coefficients.
-This explains the statement that Galerkin projection requires common-refinement geometry and a global sparse solve, whereas NN and barycentric interpolation require only local searches and independent target-point evaluations.
-
-The second-order variant, GP2, uses continuous $P^1$ source and target spaces.
-Its degrees of freedom are the original mesh vertices, and the approximation is piecewise affine on the simplicial meshes.
-The third-order variant, GP3, uses a $P^2$ target space.
-A quadratic simplex contains additional edge degrees of freedom, so the source field must also be supplied at matching quadratic accuracy.
-For a source edge with endpoints $i$ and $j$, midpoint $x_e=(x_i+x_j)/2$, edge vector $d=x_i-x_j$, and edge-averaged Hessian $\overline H_e=(H_i+H_j)/2$, the source midpoint value is reconstructed as
-\begin{equation}
-    \phi_e
-    =
-    \frac{1}{2}(\phi_i+\phi_j)
-    -\frac{1}{8}d^T\overline H_e d.
-    \label{eq:gp3-edge-value}
-\end{equation}
-For an exact quadratic field, the average of the two endpoint values differs from the midpoint value by the curvature term removed in Eq.~\eqref{eq:gp3-edge-value}; the resulting source $P^2$ representation is therefore quadratic-exact when the reconstructed Hessian is exact.
-Degree-two WLS Hessians are used in practice, and the treatment of rank-deficient stencils is reported because it can reduce the local order of GP3.
-\label{sec:galerkin-vertex-output}
-After solving the $P^2$ projection, the vertex-centered flow solver stores only the values associated with its original vertices.
-The effect of discarding the edge degrees of freedom is therefore assessed explicitly rather than treating the full $P^2$ field and the restart state as identical objects.
-
-Galerkin conservation follows directly from the projection equation rather than from an additional correction.
-Because the constant function belongs to $V_B^p$, setting $v_B=1$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:galerkin-orthogonality} gives
-\begin{equation}
-    \int_{\Omega}\phi_B^h\dd x
-    =
-    \int_{\Omega}\phi_A^h\dd x.
-    \label{eq:galerkin-conservation}
-\end{equation}
-Thus the integral of the complete finite-element field is preserved by construction, provided the intersection integration and linear solve are sufficiently accurate.
-This is a \emph{consistent} finite-element integral.
-For GP2, it also coincides with the solver's lumped nodal finite-volume integral when the control-volume weights equal the integrals of the $P^1$ basis functions.
-For vertex-only GP3 output, the consistent integral remains exact, but the lumped integral formed from vertex values alone need not be exact because part of the $P^2$ field is carried by edge degrees of freedom that are not retained by the flow solver.
-For smooth fields, this discarded contribution is expected to produce a high-order lumped defect; its order and magnitude are verified rather than assumed.
-When machine-level preservation of the solver-lumped integral is required, the bounded redistribution of Section~\ref{sec:global-correction} may be applied to the vertex-only GP3 state as an additional post-processing step.
-This optional repair does not alter the consistent conservation property of the full projected field, and its effect on pointwise accuracy is measured separately.
-The paper therefore reports both quantities.
-
-For smooth fields, GP2 and GP3 have nominal $O(h^2)$ and $O(h^3)$ approximation errors, respectively, and reproduce target-space polynomials exactly when the source representation, intersection geometry, quadrature, and linear solution are exact to the required tolerance.
-Because the restart ultimately uses nodal values, both variants are evaluated against point samples in the manufactured-field study.
-
-
-\subsubsection{Projection-level boundedness treatment}
-\label{sec:galerkin-limiter}
-
-The unconstrained $L_2$ projection is a global best approximation, but it does not satisfy a pointwise maximum principle.
-Near a discontinuity it can therefore exhibit Gibbs-type over- and undershoots even when the projection remains conservative in the consistent sense.
-Because GP2 and GP3 are obtained from the coupled system in Eq.~\eqref{eq:galerkin-system}, the node-based Taylor limiter of Eq.~\eqref{eq:limited-quadratic-reconstruction} cannot be applied directly.
-A second-stage limiter is instead applied to the projected target field.
-
-For every target vertex $j$, a local admissible interval $[\ell_j,u_j]$ is formed from the source-vertex values belonging to source simplices that overlap the target elements incident to $j$.
-Using only source vertices prevents a reconstructed quadratic edge value from enlarging the admissible interval through its own Gibbs oscillation.
-The projected value is clipped to
-\begin{equation}
-    \ell_j-\tau r
-    \leq \phi_{B,j}
-    \leq u_j+\tau r,
-    \qquad
-    r=\phi_A^{\max}-\phi_A^{\min},
-    \label{eq:galerkin-soft-bound}
-\end{equation}
-where $\tau$ is a dimensionless deadband.
-The hard limiter uses $\tau=0$ and enforces strict boundedness.
-A small positive value gives the soft limiter, allowing smooth under-resolved extrema to exceed the discrete source range by a controlled amount.
-The integral changed by clipping is subsequently redistributed among unsaturated target vertices using the bounded repair procedure of Section~\ref{sec:global-correction}.
-For GP2 this preserves the lumped and consistent integrals simultaneously when the $P^1$ basis weights coincide with the finite-volume weights.
-For vertex-only GP3, the operation preserves the selected lumped target integral but does not remove the separate contribution carried by discarded $P^2$ edge degrees of freedom unless an additional lumped-conservation repair is requested.
-Boundedness and both conservation measures are consequently reported independently.
-Bounded projection and repair strategies of this type follow the constrained Galerkin-transfer literature~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011,ShashkovWendroff2004}.
-
-\subsection{Simplicial representation of mixed-element meshes}
-\label{sec:simplicial-representation}
-
-Barycentric interpolation and Galerkin projection are formulated on triangles in two dimensions and tetrahedra in three dimensions.
-When the original grid contains quadrilaterals, pyramids, prisms, or hexahedra, each element is therefore subdivided into simplices using only its existing vertices.
-No additional interpolation nodes are introduced into the flow-solver mesh.
-The subdivision must be conforming: adjacent elements sharing a quadrilateral face must select the same face diagonal so that the resulting simplicial representation contains no hanging or mismatched interfaces.
-A deterministic rule based on the global vertex numbering is used for this purpose.
-
-The resulting approximation space is $P^p$ on the simplicial refinement, not $Q^p$ on the original quadrilateral or hexahedral elements.
-For example, a quadrilateral is represented by two triangles carrying piecewise $P^1$ or $P^2$ polynomials rather than by one bilinear or biquadratic element.
-This distinction affects the precise approximation space but not the basic conservation argument for Galerkin projection, because the refined space still contains constants.
-The chosen subdivision is kept identical for source representation, target representation, barycentric point location, and common-refinement integration so that differences between transfer families are not caused by inconsistent geometric preprocessing.
-The use of a deterministic common refinement for nonmatching meshes follows established common-refinement transfer practice~\cite{FarrellMaddison2011,JiaoHeath2004}.
-
