-\subsection{Native mesh difference and transfer-induced defect}
-\label{sec:native-transfer-decomposition}
-
-Let $U_A^k$ and $U_B^k$ be the native trajectories obtained by advancing the same physical problem from the initial condition on meshes $A$ and $B$, respectively.
-These trajectories generally differ even when no transfer is performed:
-\begin{equation}
-    U_A^{k+1}=\mathcal{S}_A(U_A^k,U_A^{k-1}),
-    \qquad
-    U_B^{k+1}=\mathcal{S}_B(U_B^k,U_B^{k-1}).
-    \label{eq:native-trajectories}
-\end{equation}
-The difference is caused by the distinct finite-volume projections, residual operators, and accumulated discretization errors of the two meshes.
-
-Suppose that the source calculation is stopped at time $t^n$ and both BDF2 history states are transferred to mesh $B$:
-\begin{equation}
-    \widehat U_B^n=\calI_{AB}U_A^n,
-    \qquad
