-\subsection{Repeated-transfer conservation drift and boundedness}
+Accuracy and boundedness do not give the same ranking. Unlimited NN2, NN3, and BAR3 have upper-plateau excursions of 0.800, 0.426, and 0.133 in the supplied density units. Barth--Jespersen limiting eliminates the reported over- and undershoots; adding GC also reduces the source-referenced integral defect to the floating-point level. Hard-limited GP2 is bounded and has a similarly small defect in this particular test. Vertex-only GP3 is bounded but retains a lumped defect of approximately $3\times10^{-4}$. The complete boundedness comparison and conservation curves are in Appendix~\ref{app:static-details}; they quantify the distinctions in Section~\ref{sec:global-correction} without establishing strict high-order boundedness for arbitrary fields.
+
+A smooth single-transfer check on the Riemann mesh gives a complementary result: GC reduces the source-referenced density defect to approximately $10^{-15}$ for all seven base operators, while changing $L_1$ error by at most 0.64\% at that resolution. This is a small measured correction cost, not a guarantee that global repair preserves the convergence order on every mesh family. Appendix~\ref{app:static-details} provides the full values and the independently verified $L_1$ refinement sequence.
