-\section{Quadratic Galerkin common-refinement verification}
+\section{Projection verification and vertex-lumping accuracy}
 \label{app:naca-gp3-verification}
 
-A constant-field patch test exposed an over-assembly error in the original two-dimensional GP3 common-refinement quadrature.
-Seven target cells in the outer mesh ring had summed source-intersection coverage greater than one, with a maximum of 2.227.
-For the affected intersection polygons, the clipper returned a signed traversal containing a self-cancelling doubled-back segment.
-The polygon shoelace area remained correct, but fan triangulation followed by the absolute value of every subtriangle area converted the cancelling lobes into positive duplicated measure.
-The load vector was consequently over-assembled while the target mass matrix retained the correct cell measure.
-
-The two-dimensional correction is to retain the signed fan-subtriangle area, consistent with the signed area and moment convention already used by the linear assembler.
-Table~\ref{tab:naca-gp3-verification} summarizes the patch and coverage checks before and after this change.
-All GP3 results in the paper use the verified assembly.
-The three-dimensional assembler is not changed because its plane-clipped sub-tetrahedra are disjoint pieces whose positive measures must be added.
+The NACA GP3 assembly used for the reported results passes constant and affine patch tests to $3.9\times10^{-11}$ and $1.1\times10^{-10}$, respectively. A mesh-specific over-coverage defect identified during verification is documented in the accompanying diagnostic table; it is not evidence of a defect in Galerkin projection itself. In two dimensions, a signed polygon traversal requires signed fan-triangle contributions. Replacing them by absolute areas can overcount self-cancelling portions of the traversal and over-assemble the load vector while leaving the target mass matrix unchanged. This convention is not transferred to disjoint three-dimensional clipped tetrahedra, whose positive measures add. Global bounded repair cannot replace coverage and polynomial-reproduction tests, because it can redistribute a local assembly error into a bounded global bias.
 
