-\section{Automated post-processing and data availability}
+\subsection{A quadratic counterexample to an automatic third-order lumped defect}
+\label{app:quadrature-mismatch}
+
+Consider $f(x)=x^2$ on $[0,1]$. Its exact $P^2$ projection onto any conforming one-dimensional mesh is itself, so there is no projection error. On a uniform mesh with spacing $h$, retain only the vertices and use the $P^1$ nodal integration weights. The resulting composite trapezoidal integral is
+\begin{equation}
+ Q_h(f)=h\left[\frac{f(0)+f(1)}{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{1/h-1}f(ih)\right]
+       =\frac13+\frac{h^2}{6}.
+ \label{eq:quadratic-counterexample}
+\end{equation}
+The difference from the consistent quadratic integral is second order despite exact nodal values and exact projection. The same result holds for $f(x,y)=x^2$ on a uniformly triangulated unit square obtained by splitting Cartesian cells; integrating the vertex-linear field in $y$ leaves the same composite rule in $x$. Two meshes can consequently have a source--target lumped difference $(h_B^2-h_A^2)/6$ even when the transferred quadratic is exact. The example is a mathematical check, not an additional CFD measurement. It rules out a general claim that vertex restriction of GP3 necessarily leaves an $O(h^3)$ lumped defect or that repairing that defect automatically retains third-order pointwise accuracy.
+
+\section{Data provenance and automated checks}
 \label{app:automated-postprocessing}
 
-The static-transfer figures and order tables are generated from the comma-separated files distributed with the manuscript.
-The script \texttt{scripts/build\_plot\_data.py} reads the complete long-form error and conservation files, selects the quantities used in the paper, recomputes the convergence orders, and writes the compact tables read directly by \texttt{pgfplots}.
-No numerical value is transcribed manually into the LaTeX source.
-
-For consecutive refinement levels, the observed order is
-\begin{equation}
-    p_{k\rightarrow k+1}
-    =
-    \frac{\log(e_k/e_{k+1})}{\log(h_k/h_{k+1})}.
-    \label{eq:pairwise-observed-order}
-\end{equation}
-A fitted order is also obtained from a least-squares line through the available $(\log h,\log e)$ points.
-The pairwise and fitted values are both retained because the short sequences include visible pre-asymptotic behavior.
-
-The supplied \texttt{Makefile} declares the original CSV files as dependencies of the generated tables and the manuscript PDF.
-Replacing an input CSV and running \texttt{make} therefore regenerates the affected tables and plots automatically.
-Existing input and generated CSV filenames and locations are preserved unless their numerical contents are changed.
+All pre-existing input and generated CSV files retain their names, locations, and numerical contents. Figures and tables read these files through \texttt{pgfplots} and \texttt{pgfplotstable}. The static-data builder recomputes fitted and consecutive orders from the supplied errors; the manuscript build tracks CSV dependencies. The additional audit script checks tabulated ratios, fits, citation keys, and preservation of the original CSV hashes. It does not rerun the flow solver.
+
+The static error/conservation CSVs are the available numerical inputs. The Riemann and NACA CSVs in this package are summaries transcribed from the supplied study reports, not replacements for the original full-precision run outputs named there. The reports are retained unchanged for provenance. The residual-summary CSV includes normalized cosine values slightly above one; these invalid angular diagnostics are preserved in the input but omitted from the numerical assessment pending recomputation. No continuous force history, pressure-shell history, state vector, or residual vector is reconstructed from isolated summary values. The editorial audit records unresolved settings, tracker inconsistencies, and diagnostics requiring original data; these must be resolved before a fully reproducible journal submission.
 
